## Program

```tcl
# tcl-lsp: stub db_eval {sql script:body}
proc p {} {
    db_eval {select 1} {puts row}
}
p
```

`tcl diag` reports one diagnostic, `W123 Unknown command 'db_eval'` on the call, and nothing on line 1. Wrapping the same line in `# tcl-lsp: stubs-begin` / `# tcl-lsp: stubs-end` gives no diagnostics at all.

## Why it is wrong

The directive line is written in the stub grammar and names the command the call needs, but outside the block it has no effect, and the only signal is a W123 on the head that the author believed they had declared. The stub page, the how-to and the annotations page all say such lines are "ignored", so the behaviour is documented, but a directive the tool recognises and then discards deserves a diagnostic of its own on the directive line, saying it sits outside a stubs block, so that the W123 can be traced to its cause. The same holds for the other directives the stub parser accepts only inside the block.

Found by the SPEC-STAB pre-release torture sweep against `rust` @ 9934c7ab2.

## What

The collision policy is reported thoroughly in every direction **except pack-vs-pack**:

| collision | reported? | where |
|---|---|---|
| same command twice in one file | yes | `pack.rs:362-375` (`already defined … this definition is ignored`) |
| same command in two files of one pack | yes | same path |
| pack command vs **shipped** command | yes | `pack::collision_notices`, `pack.rs:418-446` |
| same `file_extension` twice in one pack | yes | `loader.rs:681-686` (`redeclared; first wins`) |
| **pack A's command vs pack B's command** | **no** | — |
| **pack A's `file_extension` vs pack B's** | **no** | — |

In both unreported cases the loser is discarded and the winner is decided by the packs' **name sort order**, which is an implementation detail no author can see. `docs/design/spec-packs.md` says name collisions "are reported, shipped wins unless the pack says `-override`" — the reporting half is simply missing when both sides are packs.

This is silent, wrong-answer behaviour on the `.tclspec` loading path, so it is release-critical: a user with two packs declaring `mylib::connect` gets one of the two specs — wrong arity, wrong hover, wrong argument roles — and nothing anywhere says a choice was made.

## Repro — two packs, one command

```rust
#[test]
fn a_cross_pack_command_collision_is_reported() {
    // `alpha` sorts before `beta`, so alpha's spec is the one installed.
    let set = /* load alpha.tclspec + beta.tclspec, both workspace tier */;
    // alpha: command shared::cmd { arity 1 }
    // beta:  command shared::cmd { arity 4 }
    let reg = tcl_spectcl::install::registry_for_dialect_with_packs("tcl9.1", &set);
    assert_eq!(reg.get("shared::cmd").unwrap().arity.min, 1); // alpha won

    assert!(
        !set.notices.is_empty(),
        "the losing pack's author must be told their command was dropped"
    );
}
```

**Expected:** a notice on `beta.tclspec` naming `shared::cmd` and saying which pack won.

**Actual:** `set.notices` is empty. `beta`'s `shared::cmd` never reaches the registry and nothing is said.

Rename `alpha` to `zeta` and the winner flips, with the same silence.

## Root cause — command collisions

`rust/tcl-spectcl/src/pack.rs:453`:

```rust
pub(crate) fn installs_over(command: &PackCommand, registry: &CommandRegistry) -> bool {
    command.overrides_shipped || registry.get(command.spec.name).is_none()
}
```

`install_into` (`rust/tcl-spectcl/src/install.rs:118-128`) walks `packs.packs` in order, inserting each pack's commands. By the time `beta` is considered, `alpha` has already inserted `shared::cmd`, so `registry.get(...)` is `Some` and `installs_over` returns false — `beta`'s command is dropped on exactly the same code path a shipped-command collision takes.

The reporting counterpart, `collision_notices`, cannot see it: the server calls it against the **plain shipped registry** (`rust/tcl-lsp-server/src/lib.rs:13632`, `tcl_registry::registry_for_dialect(&dialect)`), which of course contains no pack commands. So the one function that would report the drop is handed a registry in which the collision does not exist.

Two further consequences worth deciding on deliberately:

- `-override` on `beta` makes it win instead (it is inserted, and `get` answers with the last spec registered) — but `-override` is documented as "replaces the **shipped** command of that name", not "beats another pack". Pack-vs-pack currently has no vocabulary of its own.
- Pack order is `BTreeMap` name order from `load_sources` (`pack.rs:249`), so the winner is stable across runs but arbitrary with respect to tier, path, or anything an author controls.

## Root cause — `file_extension` collisions

`rust/tcl-spectcl/src/pack.rs:171-183`:

```rust
    pub fn extension_dialects(&self) -> Vec<(String, &'static str)> {
        let mut out: Vec<(String, &'static str)> = Vec::new();
        for pack in &self.packs {
            for row in &pack.file_extensions {
                if let Some(dialect) = row.dialect
                    && !out.iter().any(|(ext, _)| *ext == row.extension)
                {
                    out.push((row.extension.clone(), dialect));
                }
            }
        }
        out
    }
```

First pack wins, no notice — while the *within-pack* duplicate of the very same row does get one (`loader.rs:681-686`). Two packs both claiming `shr` produce one routing and no explanation; the author of the losing pack sees their files detect as somebody else's dialect.

Given the one-owner-per-extension invariant, the loser here is arguably a harder error than the command case — an extension routed to the wrong dialect mis-lexes every file of that type.

## Fix shape

1. Give `collision_notices` (or a sibling) the pack-vs-pack case: walk the set accumulating claimed names, and emit a notice on the losing pack's file whenever a name is claimed twice — mirroring the in-pack duplicate notice's wording, with the winning pack named.
2. Decide and document what `-override` means between two packs; if it stays shipped-only, a second pack claiming `-override` on a name another pack already declared should be reported as the conflict it is.
3. Emit a notice from the `extension_dialects` dedup, and state the tie-break in `docs/design/spec-packs.md` rather than leaving it to `BTreeMap` order.

Related: #1626 (lazy file-extension registration) will surface the extension collision to editors, so the routing decision becomes user-visible there too.

## Containment is fine

Neither case panics, hangs, or corrupts the registry — a contested name is installed exactly once and stays enumerable exactly once, which `rust/tcl-spectcl/tests/pack_torture.rs` now pins (`two_packs_claiming_one_command_install_deterministically`, `one_owner_per_file_extension_across_packs`). This issue is about the missing notice and the arbitrary tie-break, not about stability.

