Found by the SPEC-STAB pre-release torture sweep against `rust` @ 9934c7ab2.

`docs/design/spec-packs.md` ("Compatibility policy") states the loader's central promise:

> Unknown property words, trait names, role names, or hook names are dropped with a logged notice; the rest of the spec loads.

Three drop paths in `rust/tcl-spectcl/src/loader.rs` break the "with a logged notice" half. In each case the author's command vanishes and **nothing appears in the Problems panel on the pack file** — the one surface `Backend::publish_spec_pack_notices` exists to feed. A user whose command stopped resolving has no way to find out why.

The loader never panics on any of these, so this is silent data loss rather than a crash. Filed release-critical because it impacts `.tclspec` loading.

## 1. `command NAME` with no body — dropped with no notice at all

`rust/tcl-spectcl/src/loader.rs:2985`

```rust
    let body = stmt.words.iter().skip(2).find(|w| w.braced)?;
```

The `?` returns `None` without touching `log`. Compare the empty-name case five lines above (`loader.rs:2980-2983`), which does say `command with no name dropped`.

The shape that actually bites is the classic Tcl brace-on-the-next-line mistake, which is a *syntactically valid* pack:

```
speclib demo 1.1 {
  command demo::bar
  {
    arity 1
  }
}
```

**Expected:** a notice naming `demo::bar`, e.g. ``command `demo::bar` has no body block; dropped``.

**Actual:** `demo::bar` is dropped with no mention of its name anywhere, and the orphaned block draws the notice in item 3 below.

Repro (`cargo test -p tcl-spectcl`):

```rust
#[test]
fn command_with_no_body_is_reported() {
    let pack = tcl_spectcl::loader::load_pack(
        "speclib demo 1.1 {\n  command demo::bar\n  {\n    arity 1\n  }\n}\n",
    );
    assert!(pack.command("demo::bar").is_none());
    assert!(
        pack.notices.iter().any(|n| n.message.contains("demo::bar")),
        "the dropped command must be named: {:#?}",
        pack.notices
    );
}
```

## 2. A second `speclib` block in one file is dropped with no notice

`rust/tcl-spectcl/src/loader.rs:636-643`

```rust
    let Some(speclib) = top.iter().find(|s| s.word_text(0) == "speclib") else { ... };
    for stray in top.iter().filter(|s| s.word_text(0) != "speclib") {
        log.unknown_property(stray);
    }
```

`find` takes the first `speclib`; the stray loop then filters *out* every other `speclib`, so a second one and every command inside it are discarded in silence. Concatenating two packs into one file — the obvious thing to do when tidying up, and something `cat a.tclspec b.tclspec > merged.tclspec` produces — loses the second half.

**Expected:** either both blocks load (a file may declare more than one pack), or the extra block draws a notice naming what was dropped.

**Actual:** `two::b` and `two::c` below are gone, `pack.notices` is empty.

```rust
#[test]
fn a_second_speclib_block_is_reported() {
    let pack = tcl_spectcl::loader::load_pack(
        "speclib one 1.1 {\n command one::a { arity 1 }\n}\n\
         speclib two 1.1 {\n command two::b { arity 1 }\n command two::c { arity 1 }\n}\n",
    );
    assert_eq!(pack.name, "one");
    assert!(
        !pack.notices.is_empty(),
        "the discarded second `speclib` block must be reported"
    );
}
```

## 3. The orphaned-block notice prints the entire block body as the "property name"

`rust/tcl-spectcl/src/loader.rs:182-185`

```rust
    fn unknown_property(&mut self, stmt: &Stmt) {
        let word = stmt.word_text(0);
        self.say(stmt.line, format!("unknown property `{word}` dropped"));
    }
```

`word_text(0)` on a braced block is *every byte between the braces*, so the pack in item 1 produces this notice, verbatim, as a **diagnostic message in the Problems panel**:

```text
unknown property `
    arity 1
  ` dropped
```

A multi-line diagnostic message renders as one long unreadable line in the Problems panel, and says nothing about what the user actually did wrong.

**Expected:** a bounded, single-line message — e.g. ``unknown property (a `{…}` block with no preceding declaration) dropped``, or the first word of the block with an ellipsis.

**Actual:** the whole block body, newlines included, interpolated into the message.

## Fix shape

All three are small and local:

1. `loader.rs:2985` — replace the bare `?` with a logged `else` naming `name`.
2. `loader.rs:636` — collect every top-level `speclib`, and either load them all or report the ones not taken.
3. `loader.rs:182-185` — truncate/normalise `word` before interpolation; a block word should be summarised, never inlined.

The regression tests belong beside `rust/tcl-spectcl/tests/pack_torture.rs`, which covers the containment half of this surface (no panic, no hang, no stack overflow on any of these inputs — that part all holds).

## Not affected

For the record, the same sweep confirmed these *are* reported correctly: empty command name, unknown words at every level, bogus `-dialect` names, invalid lifecycle orderings at command and option level, lifecycle containment violations, duplicate commands within and across a pack's files, unreadable/non-UTF-8 files, and shipped-command collisions.

## Related

- #1635 — a BOM-prefixed pack loading nothing, in the same loader.

