Given the following code

```tcl
proc myProc {} {
    if {[info exists ACCESS_RPC_HANDLE]} {
        ...
    }
}
```
tcl-irule will raise a linter warning:
> Variable 'ACCESS_RPC_HANDLE' is read before it is set

Found after working through real-library autoload failures in #2188. Tcl 9 package.tcl uses `$opts(-$key)` as the inner foreach list while generating package ifneeded scripts.

Reduced native witness:
```tcl
proc p {} {
    array set opts {-load {} -source foo}
    set out {}
    foreach key {load source} {
        foreach filespec $opts(-$key) {
            lappend out [list $key $filespec]
        }
    }
    return $out
}
puts [p]
```
C Tcl 9.0.4 returns `{source foo}`. The baseline VM (`--tcl-version 9.0`) returns `{load {$opts(-$key)}} {source {$opts(-$key)}}`, treating the dynamic array reference itself as a literal list.

Investigate canonical variable-word/list-source facts through foreach lowering and emission. Preserve dynamic array-index substitution and avoid consumer-side textual reparsing. Cover direct and nested loops, braced literal controls, and side-effectful index evaluation against C9. Do not patch package.tcl or skip the portable package tests.

This is distinct from child interpreter startup and qualified alias registration. Taking it as a focused compiler follow-up.

## Summary

Design and implement the event/framework layer that sits above the hardened BPF-IR work in #1202 and the Linux kernel target in #1203.

Today BPF-Tcl recognises only `SOCKET_FILTER`/`SOCKET` and `XDP`. A `when` block becomes an independent program, priority only sorts module/CLI indexes, and `attach` is descriptive metadata. There is no dispatcher or link ordering, loader lifecycle, event-specific context schema, ring buffer, or operational status model.

## Goals

- Make every event a registry-described contract, rather than another string-match arm.
- Give multiple handlers explicit, testable composition semantics.
- Load, attach, inspect, replace, and detach programs safely.
- Add typed kernel-to-userspace event records for observability families.
- Keep the default workflow non-destructive: inspect and test-run before live attachment.

## Event schema

Introduce an `EventSpec` (or equivalent registry descriptor) containing:

- canonical name and aliases;
- Linux BPF program type and ELF section convention;
- target context type and typed readable fields;
- permitted low-level operations and helper capabilities;
- verdict algebra, default verdict, and terminal/non-terminal behaviour;
- attachment parameter schema;
- minimum kernel/BTF/features;
- whether the event emits packet decisions, userspace records, or both.

Initial schemas should retain XDP and socket filters, then add TC ingress/egress and cgroup connect/bind. Tracepoints, kprobes, and uprobes follow once typed ring-buffer records exist. LSM remains deferred until stronger policy and deployment guarantees exist.

## Handler composition

Define source semantics before choosing an implementation:

- lower priority numbers run first;
- a terminal verdict stops evaluation;
- continuing must be explicit (for example, a typed `next` outcome);
- each event declares its default when no handler terminates;
- shared maps have declared ownership and compatibility rules.

Compile handlers either into one dispatcher or a verified program chain. The implementation must preserve the source ordering and verdict rules; sorting independent object files is not sufficient.

## Scoped configuration

Replace the current single global profile, capability policy, and attach declaration with explicit module, event, and handler scopes. Reject ambiguous declarations and show the resolved configuration in `check` output.

## Loader and link lifecycle

Provide a loader surface along these lines:

- `plan`/`check`: show programs, maps, required kernel features, attach targets, and expected changes;
- `load`: verifier-load programs and create maps without live attachment;
- `test-run`: execute supported program types through `BPF_PROG_TEST_RUN`;
- `attach`: create BPF links or the safest type-specific equivalent;
- `status`: report program IDs/tags, links, maps, pins, and source identity;
- `detach`: remove only resources owned by the deployment.

Requirements:

- use BPF links where available;
- use unique, ownership-labelled pin paths;
- support atomic replacement or rollback on partial failure;
- refuse to replace unrelated links/pins;
- make dry-run the obvious path;
- clean temporary resources after failed loads;
- never attach to a live interface as part of an ordinary compiler test.

## Userspace event channel

Add schema-generated records for trace/observability events:

- explicit field types, sizes, alignment, and byte order;
- versioned record headers;
- ring-buffer transport first, with loss counters;
- a structured JSON/text consumer;
- back-pressure and record-drop behaviour documented.

## Testing

- Unit tests for event lookup, aliases, context fields, capabilities, verdicts, and configuration scope.
- Composition tests proving order, continuation, terminal verdicts, defaults, and shared-map behaviour.
- Kernel verifier and `BPF_PROG_TEST_RUN` tests for every supported program family.
- Root-only integration tests in disposable network namespaces/veth pairs or isolated cgroups.
- Attach, replace, rollback, status, and detach tests that assert no links, pins, maps, or interfaces leak.
- Ring-buffer schema/version and loss-accounting tests.

## Real-world acceptance scenarios

1. XDP drops a blocked destination port and counts the decision in a shared map.
2. Two XDP handlers compose deterministically: an early deny handler terminates, while an audit handler can continue.
3. A TC handler marks or redirects traffic in a disposable namespace.
4. A cgroup policy denies a selected connect target for one isolated test cgroup.
5. A tracepoint handler emits typed process/event records to a JSON consumer.
6. An interrupted or failed deployment rolls back without disturbing unrelated host BPF state.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Events are declared through typed registry data, not command/event-name branches in consumers.
- [ ] Priority and continuation have documented source semantics and generated deployment semantics.
- [ ] Configuration scopes are explicit and malformed/ambiguous input is rejected.
- [ ] The loader supports plan, verifier load, test-run, attach, status, replacement/rollback, and detach.
- [ ] Resource ownership prevents accidental replacement or cleanup of unrelated BPF state.
- [ ] Typed ring-buffer records have a versioned ABI and observable loss accounting.
- [ ] XDP, socket filter, TC, and cgroup scenarios run in isolated Linux integration tests.
- [ ] Every integration test proves cleanup.
- [ ] User documentation includes Debian/Ubuntu dependencies, build commands, privilege requirements, and runnable examples.

## Dependencies

- #1202 defines the registry-described low-level language and proof-bearing BPF-IR contracts.
- #1203 provides Linux context lowering, verifier-safe packet access, maps/relocations, and verifier tests.
